From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7663: accept the 0x7653 efuse of the Echo Dot
 (5th gen)

The MT7663 on the Echo Dot (5th gen) reads 0x7653 as the chip ID in its
efuse and one TX path in HW_CONF1. mt76 passed the efuse to the firmware as
is, and the radio never received anything: no false CCA, no frame in monitor
mode on any 2.4 or 5 GHz channel, scans empty. Given the same efuse with the
ID 0x7663 it received (35 networks), and with both TX paths enabled it also
associated, at -54 dBm and 72 Mbit/s (C2N6L4, 2026-11-26). Amazon's driver
runs the chip with one spatial stream on both paths. Correct the ID and
enable both paths for this efuse.

---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7615/eeprom.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7615/eeprom.c
@@ -330,6 +330,25 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * The Echo Dot (5th gen)'s MT7663 carries 0x7653 in its efuse chip ID and one
+ * TX path in HW_CONF1. With the ID as is, the firmware ignored the EEPROM
+ * buffer and the receiver stayed deaf: no false CCA, no frame in monitor
+ * mode. With the ID 0x7663 and one TX path it received but could not
+ * associate; with both paths it associated (C2N6L4, 2026-11-26).
+ */
+static void mt7663_eeprom_fixup(struct mt7615_dev *dev)
+{
+	u8 *eep = dev->mt76.eeprom.data;
+
+	if (!is_mt7663(&dev->mt76) || get_unaligned_le16(eep) != 0x7653)
+		return;
+
+	put_unaligned_le16(0x7663, eep);
+	eep[MT7663_EE_HW_CONF1] &= ~MT_EE_HW_CONF1_TX_MASK;
+	eep[MT7663_EE_HW_CONF1] |= FIELD_PREP(MT_EE_HW_CONF1_TX_MASK, 2);
+}
+
 int mt7615_eeprom_init(struct mt7615_dev *dev, u32 addr)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -347,6 +366,7 @@
 		mt7615_cal_free_data(dev);
 	}
 
+	mt7663_eeprom_fixup(dev);
 	mt7615_eeprom_parse_hw_cap(dev);
 	memcpy(dev->mphy.macaddr, dev->mt76.eeprom.data + MT_EE_MAC_ADDR,
 	       ETH_ALEN);
